Short answer: not quite — it fails LRU semantics and has a length bug.

Problems in your code:
- get() doesn't update recency. Reading a key must make it most-recently-used.
- put() increments length even when updating an existing key (should not).
- put() may evict when overwriting an existing key (shouldn't).
- You don't need a separate length field; use map.size.

Fixed implementation (replaces the class in your file):
````typescript {/home/girgis/repo/learning/150LeetCode/ts/medium/LRUCache.ts}
// ...existing code...
class LRUCache {
	capacity: number;
	mp: Map<number, number>;
	constructor(capacity: number) {
		this.capacity = capacity;
		this.mp = new Map();
	}

	get(key: number): number {
		if (!this.mp.has(key)) return -1;
		const val = this.mp.get(key)!;
		// move to most-recent position
		this.mp.delete(key);
		this.mp.set(key, val);
		return val;
	}

	put(key: number, value: number): void {
		// if key exists, remove it so set() will move it to most-recent
		if (this.mp.has(key)) {
			this.mp.delete(key);
		} else if (this.mp.size === this.capacity) {
			// evict least-recent (first inserted)
			const firstKey = this.mp.keys().next().value!;
			this.mp.delete(firstKey);
		}
		this.mp.set(key, value);
	}
}
// ...existing code...
````

This uses Map insertion order: deleting and re-setting a key moves it to the end (most recent). Complexity: O(1) amortized per operation.

